# {{.AppID}}

This is a [Wendy](https://docs.wendy.dev/latest/) app. Wendy is an open source operating system and developer toolchain for robots, drones, and edge AI devices; the `wendy` CLI builds this project and deploys it to a device over USB-C, the local network, or Wendy Cloud.

{{if .Lite -}}
It targets Wendy Lite on an ESP32 microcontroller and is written in {{.LanguageName}}. Wendy Lite supports only the `network` entitlement.
{{- else if .Mac -}}
It targets Wendy Agent for Mac as a native macOS process, not a Linux container, and is written in {{.LanguageName}}. Native macOS apps do not use WendyOS container entitlements.
{{- else -}}
It targets WendyOS, running as a container on a Linux edge device such as an NVIDIA Jetson or Raspberry Pi, and is written in {{.LanguageName}}.
{{- end}}
`wendy.json` is the app manifest (app ID `{{.AppID}}`); `wendy json schema` prints its JSON Schema.

## Commands

Run these in this directory. `wendy <command> --help` is the authority on flags. Output is JSON when stdout is not a terminal; `--json` forces it.

- Deploy: `wendy run` builds the app, deploys it to the selected device, and streams its logs. From a non-interactive shell, {{if .Linux}}`wendy run --detach --wait-ready` returns once the app is ready and fails if it crashes{{else}}`wendy run --detach` returns after deploying{{end}}.{{if not .Lite}} `wendy run --watch` redeploys on every save.{{end}}
{{- if .Linux}}
- Build only: `wendy build`
{{- end}}
- Find devices: `wendy discover --json` scans nearby devices once. For Wendy Cloud devices, sign in with `wendy auth login`, then run `wendy cloud discover`.
- Choose the device: pass `--device <host[:port]>` to any command, set `WENDY_DEVICE` for one shell, or save a default with `wendy device set-default <device>` (`wendy device get-default` shows it). Non-interactive shells get no device picker, so choose one before deploying.
{{- if .Lite}}
- Logs: `wendy device logs` streams the firmware console.
{{- else if .Linux}}
- Logs: `wendy device logs --app {{.AppID}} --tail 20 --no-follow` prints recent logs and exits; without `--no-follow` it keeps streaming.
- Apps on the device: `wendy device apps list`, then `wendy device apps stop|start|remove {{.AppID}}`.
- Device details: `wendy device info` (OS, architecture, GPU, NPU).
{{- end}}
- Offline docs: `wendy docs` lists topics and `wendy docs <topic>` prints one.
{{- if .Linux}}

## Entitlements

The app can use only the hardware and network access that `wendy.json` grants. Change entitlements with the CLI, which validates them:

- `wendy project add gpu` (also `camera`, `audio`, `usb`, `bluetooth`, `gpio`, `i2c`, `serial`, and more; `wendy project add --help` lists them all)
- `wendy project add persist --name data --path /data` for storage that survives restarts
- `wendy project remove <name>`, `wendy project show`, and `wendy project validate` (run it before deploying)
{{- end}}

## MCP server

`wendy mcp setup` adds the Wendy MCP server and Wendy skills to Claude Code, Claude Desktop, Codex, Cursor, and Windsurf; restart the tool afterwards. Call `wendy_status` first, find a target with `device_list`, connect with `device_connect`, then deploy with `run`, passing this directory's absolute path as `project_path`. A started app is not a working app: check `container_list` and `telemetry_logs`, then test the app itself.

